<!-- Local MMCA.Common source override, emitted because you asked for the local-mmca option.
     (Option names are spelled without their leading dashes throughout this file: XML forbids a
     double hyphen inside a comment, and a file that breaks that rule is not valid XML at all, so
     MSBuild never imports it and the override silently does nothing.)

     It swaps every MMCA.Common.* PackageReference for a ProjectReference under LocalMMCAPath (the
     swap itself lives in Directory.Build.targets), so this app builds against framework source
     instead of the published nuget.org packages. It needs ..\MMCA.Common\Source to exist on disk.

     After editing MMCA.Common source, rebuild MMCA.Common in Debug BEFORE this app, or the IDE
     binds the stale last-built Debug reference assembly and reports phantom CS0103 errors against
     the members you just added. It is not a code bug.

     A local source-mode build can also pass where a package-mode Release build fails on an
     analyzer, so treat a green local build as necessary, not sufficient.

     Delete this file to go back to consuming the published packages. It is gitignored. -->
<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <UseLocalMMCA>true</UseLocalMMCA>
    <LocalMMCAPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)..\MMCA.Common\Source\</LocalMMCAPath>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
